---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BreakinTheLawChart from "../components/BreakinTheLawChart.astro";
import FontAwesomeIcon from "../components/FontAwesomeIcon.astro";
import SingleArticle from "../components/SingleArticle.astro";
import TrainingChart from "../components/TrainingChart.astro";
import { faStrava } from "@fortawesome/free-brands-svg-icons";
import { formatPageTitle, getPostPage } from "../lib/posts";
import type { PostMetadata } from "../lib/posts";

export const metadata: PostMetadata = {
  title: "Marji 2026",
  date: "2026-09-17",
  kind: "single",
  cover: "/images/marji-2026-hero.png",
  draft: true,
};

// todo: this doesn't make sense anymore
// external fn to read `metadata`` above?
const { post, draftRedirect } = getPostPage(metadata, Astro.redirect);
if (draftRedirect) {
  return draftRedirect;
}
---

<BaseLayout title={formatPageTitle(post)}>
  <SingleArticle post={post}>
    <p>
      I try to visit Marquette annually. It's my favorite place to ride in
      Michigan. Over the years, I kept hearing about the Marji race. It's a
      ridiculously hard, point-to-point race spanning three awesome trail
      systems. But, I hadn't ever rode my mountain bike more than 70 miles on
      mostly flat ground. It seemed impossible, yet I kept thinking about it.
    </p>
    <p>
      After finding myself with some unexpected free time between 2024 and 2026,
      I really upped my time on the bike. In 2024, my "long-rides" were about 30
      to 40 miles. In 2025, I started with my regular 30 to 40 mile rides, but
      around July, I increasted to about 50 to 70 miles. There really wasn't a
      goal at the time. I was more exploring what endurance rides were like.
    </p>
    <TrainingChart />
    <p>
      todo: this is rough. work on timeline of getting serious about tech/pace.
      then incorporate fear of rocks and rising to the occasion (top of the
      world/no dab).
    </p>
    <p>
      I've wanted to be competent at things like scary trail for years, but I
      didn't really know how to safely progress my skills with the limited
      terrain in Traverse City. I started watching the "How Not to Mountain
      Bike" series by Ben Cathro. During each ride I was intentional to try
      working on skills I saw in the videos. From 2023 to 2026 I was able to
      move from the tp 50% to top 10 of all time on my local favorite segment
      Breaking the Law. It's unreal.
      <BreakinTheLawChart />
    </p>
    <p>
      In September 2025 I visited Marquette and had an absolute blast. I focused
      on a lot of the tech features I had always been afraid to ride.
      <br />
      look up segment progress from previous years (esp. doctors).
    </p>
    <p>
      The first hurdle is registering for the event. If you've never tried, it's
      a foundational piece of the experience. There is a lot of teasing about
      when registration will open. A lot of this is misinformation designed for
      maximum torture. Their registration service, BikeReg, doesn't handle
      concurrency very well, causing angst and strife. The event sells out
      within minutes of opening. As I went thru the registration process, there
      were a lot of popups and messages along the way. I knew I was fighting the
      clock, so I YOLO'd some of the answers thinking it'd be easier to fix
      things after if I had to.
    </p>
    <p>
      I was feeling pretty good about getting into the race. I had also been
      working really hard at trying to improve my downhill skills for the year.
      Silly me ended up having a
      <a href="https://www.strava.com/activities/16593554884">crash</a> on November
      28, 2025. There had been a snow the day before and I misjudged where the berm
      was supposed to be causing me to smash my shoulder into the top going 18+ mph.
      This was the first proper crash I'd had where I couldn't brush myself off and
      get home OK. There wasn't a way to put weight on my right arm the whole way
      home. What is interesting about this crash is that I was able to continue riding
      after a few days. It was confusing though because some arm positions were incredibly
      weak. After a few weeks of thinking I was healing and getting progressivly worse,
      I admitted defeat and got checked by my doctor. The good news: nothing was broken.
      The bad news: it can take longer to heal fleshy bits than bone in cases like
      this. This was not good. Marji was at risk. I had a spring trip to Brevard,
      NC planned. Basically, all was ruined.
    </p>
    <p>
      In January 2026, I started Physical Therapy (PT) and was told my rotator
      cuff was pretty bad. By that time I couldn't lift my arm over my head
      anymore. I had to start with the little pink 2 lbs weight for some of the
      exercises. If you've never experienced PT before, I highly recommend.
      There are a couple of touchpoint visits per week along with homework. I
      took the home training quite seriously doing all of them above and beyond,
      and it really paid off. After 7 weeks of PT things were getting better!
    </p>
    <p>
      My previous "training" was basically do a couple of short rides during the
      week and one hero effort on the weekend. For me, hero was defined as 4+
      hours at max effort. It just wasn't working. I kept hitting a wall and
      would quickly lose steam. It's also clear from my heart rate and hourly
      average speed things fell apart after 4 hours. I needed more information.
    </p>
    <p>
      In March 2026, I started researching endurance bike training plans. I read
      a lot about other people's experiences and found that for beginners,
      consistency was the best plan. If we are specifically training endurance,
      becoming more efficient in Zone 2 is key. This is a massive
      oversimplification, but it's a framework that I could iterate and review
      progress. This is also counter to the approach I'd been using over the
      last few years where I put everything I had into a weekend rip. At this
      point I became really excited to see if I could push past my limits.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ok, so now my training plan was to just ride my bike a lot. Easy. Try not
      to take multiple days off, check. I knew the course is about 110 miles
      with 13,000 feet of elevation, usually technical, punchy stuff. That was a
      problem for later.
    </p>
    <p>
      It was hard to get into a consistent training rhythm. It wasn't that I
      didn't want to ride, but rather, the trails felt boring after a trip to
      Brevard, NC in the spring. Nasty technical trails are my favorite. Give me
      rocks, roots, and drops. I'm not interested in climbing unless it's
      technical (aka <a href="https://www.trailforks.com/trails/zone-4/"
        >zone 4</a
      >) or there's a fun downhill on the other side. My training plans called
      for a lot of zone 2 work, but in my heart, I wanted to live the downhill
      life. So how do I make this work?
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a
        href="https://www.traversecitymi.gov/community/recreation-directory/grand-traverse-commons/grand-traverse-commons.html"
        >Grand Traverse Commons Area</a
      > has some sections like old orchard climb and root hill which fit tech climbs.
      There are a few flow trails as well. As an aside, I got back into mountain biking
      too late (2017) to really progress to the level of downhill I want, but I still
      give it my best.
    </p>
    <p>
      By adjusting things like speed, cadence and route I was able to find ways
      to make the commons work for most of my training needs. The only gap was
      distance. I had been riding out to the unmarked VASA trails to work on
      that part. The city started a large infrastructure project that made it
      annoying to get across town. I am always looking for reasons to give up,
      so it was perfect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Luckily, I was able to start stringing a few sections in town sections
      together. This solved the distance issue and saved me from having to spend
      an additional hour and a half riding to &amp; from the woods.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fast-forward to August 2026. I was feeling really good about my progress.
      According to strava I had climbed 60% more than the previous year. I had
      40% more riding time and distance as well. I finished the Lumberjack 100
      with a sub-10 hour from last place. I also was starting to make Strava Top
      10 trophies on some of the segments I loved. That was the most shocking
      part- I never thought it'd be possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conclusion of my 2026 season happened on <FontAwesomeIcon
        icon={faStrava}
        class="strava-link-icon"
      /><a href="https://www.strava.com/activities/19953738369">August 29</a>. I
      had been riding the chickenleg 100 course and misjudged an obstacle on
      course. This wasn't an intentional feature, but rather a rock that had
      rolled into the middle of the trail. I ended up going Over The Bars (OTB)
      and landed on my shoulder causing a <a href="/clavicle-fracture"
        >mildly displaced midclavicular fracture</a
      >.
    </p>
  </SingleArticle>
</BaseLayout>
